{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-32918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}\\,,\\,{e}^{t}\\,,\\,2{e}^{t}⟩$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-32918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that $r({t}_{0})=c\\,{r}^{′}({t}_{0}).$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =c\\,{r}^{′}(t) \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =c\\,⟨4t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ ⟨2{t}^{2},\\,{e}^{t},\\,2{e}^{t}⟩ & =⟨4ct,\\,c{e}^{t},\\,2c{e}^{t}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2}=4ct \\\\ {e}^{t}=c{e}^{t} \\\\ 2{e}^{t}=2c{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second and third equations, we obtain that $c=1.$ Substituting into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{t}^{2} & =1\\cdot 4t \\\\ {t}^{2} & =2t \\\\ {t}^{2}-2t & =0 \\\\ t(t-2) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $r$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the points corresponding to $t=0$ and $2.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨2(0)^{2},\\,{e}^{0},\\,2{e}^{0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,2⟩ \\\\ r(2) & =⟨2(2)^{2},\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨8,\\,{e}^{2},\\,2{e}^{2}⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the points $(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2}).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-32918" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bf882978d8b075256a354c0dd5eacd4cca6c4758827f5db5d0ae9f7f9e00a35"} {"question" "q-32918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-32918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "$(0,1,1)$ and $(4,e,2e)$"] ["text" "$(0,1,1)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2})$"] ["text" "$(0,1,2)$ and $(8,e,2e)$"] ["text" "$(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2})$"] ["text" "$(1,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2})$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-32918" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "$(0,1,2)$ and $(8,{e}^{2},2{e}^{2})$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-75203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨t,\\,{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-75203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=0$,  the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨0,\\,{e}^{0},\\,{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,\\,{e}^{0},\\,-{e}^{-0}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point \n where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-75203" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bf882978d8b075256a354c0dd5eacd4cca6c4758827f5db5d0ae9f7f9e00a35"} {"question" "q-75203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-75203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,-1,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,1,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,0,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨-1,\\,-1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨0,\\,-1,\\,1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] ["math" "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-75203" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨0,\\,1,\\,1⟩+u⟨1,\\,1,\\,-1⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following gives the equation of the tangent to the curve $r(t)=⟨3t+5,1-{t}^{3}⟩$ at the point where $t=0,$  valid for all  real values of $u?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "At the point where $t=0,$ the vector ${r}^{′}(0)$ is tangent to the curve, so the tangent line to the curve at $P$ can be parametrized as\n \n\n$$\nR(u)=r(0)+u{r}^{′}(0),\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty )\n$$\n\n \n\nFirst, we find $r(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(0) & =⟨3(0)+5,1-{0}^{3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨5,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t+5),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we evaluate ${r}^{′}(t)$ at $t=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0) & =⟨3,-3(0)^{2}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the tangent line to the given curve at the point where $t=0$ is\n\n$$\nR(u)=⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩,\\,u\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bf882978d8b075256a354c0dd5eacd4cca6c4758827f5db5d0ae9f7f9e00a35"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩"] ["math" "⟨3,-1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩"] ["math" "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨1,5⟩"] ["math" "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,-3⟩"] ["math" "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126835" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "⟨5,1⟩+u⟨3,0⟩"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2{t}^{2}-t,5-3{t}^{2}⟩,$ find the point at which the tangent to the curve is parallel to the vector $⟨-1,2⟩.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5-3{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨4t-1,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $⟨-1\\,,\\,2⟩$ are parallel for some specific value ${t}_{0}$ of the parameter $t$ if and only if there exists a constant $c$ such that ${r}^{′}({t}_{0})=c⟨-1,2⟩.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =c⟨-1,2⟩ \\\\ ⟨4t-1,-6t⟩ & =⟨-c,2c⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the components gives:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}4t-1=-c \\\\ -6t=2c\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we obtain that $c=-4t+1.$ Substituting $c=-4t+1$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6t & =2(-4t+1) \\\\ -6t & =-8t+2 \\\\ 2t & =2 \\\\ t & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}$ are parallel only at the point corresponding to $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =⟨2(1)^{2}-1,5-3(1)^{2}⟩=⟨1,2⟩,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe conclude that $⟨-1,2⟩$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are parallel at the point $(1,2).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126902" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bf882978d8b075256a354c0dd5eacd4cca6c4758827f5db5d0ae9f7f9e00a35"} {"question" "q-126902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(0,5)"] ["math" "(1,2)"] ["math" "(10,-7)"] ["math" "(3,2)"] ["math" "(6,-7)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-126902" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(1,2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-164840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given the curve $r(t)=({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j,$ $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, find all of the points at which ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $r(t)$ are perpendicular." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-164840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we calculate the derivative of $r(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,j \\\\  & =2t\\,i+2t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe vectors $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular for some value of the parameter $t$ if and only if $r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t)=0.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t)\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ [({t}^{2}-2)\\,i+{t}^{2}\\,j]\\cdot (2t\\,i+2t\\,j) & =0 \\\\ ({t}^{2}-2)\\cdot 2t+{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t & =0 \\\\ 2t(2{t}^{2}-2) & =0 \\\\ 4t({t}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ 4t(t-1)(t+1) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $t\\in (0,\\infty )$, the only solution is $t=1.$\n\nFinally, since\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(1) & =((1)^{2}-2)\\,i+(1)^{2}\\,j=-\\,i+j,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n we conclude that $r(t)$ and ${r}^{′}(t)$ are perpendicular at the point $(-1,1).$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1529a68006fe390e6ff3f9b959"} {"question" "q-164840" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "0bf882978d8b075256a354c0dd5eacd4cca6c4758827f5db5d0ae9f7f9e00a35"} {"question" "q-164840" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14063843/state.json" "file_sha256" "cf4c6518b7f421adeeb07796743e800af979bd1